AG2-PACT v0.3

Perturbation-Aware Cell-State Testing of Conditional Genomic Foundation Models

A preregisterable research programme — verification-corrected draft

Author: Ouadi Maakoul and SI TEAM
Version: 0.3 — November 2026
Status: G1.1–G1.5 frozen subject to the implementation notes below. No evaluation-set predictions inspected.

---

Abstract

AlphaGenome 2 is a conditional genomic foundation model released on 11 November 2026. It takes a target biological condition — a tissue, cell type, or cell state — as input at every stage of its architecture, rather than predicting thousands of tracks in parallel as its predecessor did. It is trained on more than 107 million individual cell profiles across 68 tissues from human and mouse, supports cross-modality imputation from as little as one measured modality, and reports on the TenK10K immune-cell benchmark a causal-variant identification rate of 30 % (vs 16 % for AlphaGenome 1 fine-tuned on single-cell data) and correct direction of gene effect in 43 % of cases (vs 23 %).

These are developer-reported results on developer-selected benchmarks. They do not establish that the model's predictions remain reliable under experimental perturbation, across cell-state mismatches, or at genomic distances where regulatory interactions are hardest to model.

This proposal specifies AG2-PACT, an independent validation and evidence framework whose central falsifiable question is:

Can cell-state-aware evidence provenance and distance-dependent reliability assessment improve the experimental validation efficiency of conditional genomic foundation-model predictions beyond raw sequence-to-function scores?

The framework separates two non-equivalent evaluation problems. Track A evaluates regulatory predictions against experimentally compatible variant-effect estimands (eQTL, caQTL, sQTL, MPRA). Track B evaluates whether model-derived regulatory predictions anticipate experimental perturbation responses (CRISPRi, CRISPRa, TAP-seq, Perturb-seq). Because variant effects and perturbation effects are different biological quantities, Track B uses only a finite, pre-specified set of transformations or, where no transformation is defensible, restricts itself to prioritization analysis without quantitative calibration claims.

Four hypotheses address distance-dependent magnitude miscalibration, cell-state conditioning, modality provenance, and experimental prioritization. The project is falsification-first: dataset independence, licensing, statistical power and model reproducibility are established before any evaluation-set prediction is inspected. Negative, inconclusive and equivalence results are reportable outcomes.

---

1. Introduction

1.1 The conditional shift and the evaluation problem it creates

AlphaGenome 1 predicted thousands of functional genomic tracks in parallel from a 1 Mb DNA sequence. The parallel approach spread model capacity thin and made it difficult to capture subtle differences between closely related cell types. AlphaGenome 2 replaces parallel prediction with conditional prediction: a single biological condition is supplied at every architectural stage, and the model predicts how the same stretch of DNA is read in that condition.

This architectural shift creates three evaluation problems that aggregate benchmark performance cannot resolve.

Problem 1 — Context is now an input, and inputs can be mismatched. A conditional model's prediction quality may depend on whether the supplied cell-state context is the right context. TenK10K performance (30 % causal variant identification) is reported on a benchmark the developers selected; whether matched cell-state conditioning improves performance on independent held-out evidence is an open empirical question.

Problem 2 — Imputation is now a first-class capability, and imputed predictions may differ in reliability. AlphaGenome 2 separates a cell's biological state from its outputs, so if a scientist inputs only one measurement, the model predicts the missing experimental data. The API release notes confirm a filter_to_observed helper on TrackData and JunctionData — the operational primitive by which observed and imputed tracks can be distinguished. Whether imputed modality predictions are as reliable as observed-modality predictions is the subject of H3.

Problem 3 — Perturbation is not variation. CRISPRi ablates or strongly suppresses a regulatory element; a variant modulates it. A variant may weaken an enhancer by 20 %, while CRISPRi may suppress it far more strongly, non-proportionally, and with context-dependent effects not represented in the variant score. Treating the difference between a variant score and a perturbation log-fold change as "calibration error" would be a category error dressed as a metric.

1.2 What this proposal does not claim

This proposal does not claim that AlphaGenome 2 is unreliable. It does not claim that its reported gains are incorrect. It does not claim that a structured evidence layer must improve on raw model scores. It claims only that these are testable questions, and it specifies the tests, the decision rules, and the conditions under which the framework's own contribution would be falsified.

---

2. Objectives

O1 — Predictive validity. Evaluate direction, ranking and magnitude agreement between model predictions and experimentally compatible measurements, keeping the three properties mathematically separate.

O2 — Contextual reliability. Test whether matched cell-state conditioning improves predictive validity on held-out evidence, against appropriate controls rather than against "no conditioning" as a straw baseline.

O3 — Modality provenance. Determine whether observed-versus-imputed modality status predicts differences in model reliability, using the filter_to_observed primitive where it is operational and controlled modality ablation where the API supports it.

O4 — Distance-dependent calibration. Test whether prediction errors exhibit a systematic relationship with genomic distance, and whether this relationship differs between AlphaGenome 1 and AlphaGenome 2, using equivalence testing rather than significance-only inference.

O5 — Experimental utility. Determine whether a structured evidence score improves diversity-constrained experimental candidate prioritization compared with raw model scores, with a component ablation that can attribute or refuse to attribute utility to provenance.

O6 — Reproducibility. Produce an independent, documented, reproducible evaluation framework subject to applicable API and dataset licensing.

