3. Conceptual architecture

Three analytical layers, kept mathematically separate and integrated only at Layer III. A layer that appears to work only because it is entangled with another layer is not a demonstrated contribution.

3.1 Layer I — Predictive validity

Let \hat{y}_{v,c,m} denote the model-predicted effect for variant v, cell state c, and modality m. Let y^{\text{exp}}_{v,c} denote the corresponding experimental measurement, where and only where the experimental estimand is compatible with the model output.

Direction agreement.

\mathrm{DA} = \frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N} \mathbb{1}\!\left[\operatorname{sign}(\hat{y}_i)=\operatorname{sign}(y_i^{\text{exp}})\right].

Ranking agreement.

\rho_s = \operatorname{Spearman}(\hat{y},y^{\text{exp}}).

Magnitude agreement.

\mathrm{MAE} = \frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}|\hat{y}_i-y_i^{\text{exp}}|,
\qquad
\mathrm{RMSE} = \sqrt{\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}(\hat{y}_i-y_i^{\text{exp}})^2}.

Calibration — through-origin and with intercept.

y^{\text{exp}} = \beta\hat{y}+\epsilon \qquad \text{(through origin, comparable to prior work)},

y^{\text{exp}} = \alpha+\beta\hat{y}+\epsilon \qquad \text{(with intercept)}.

The through-origin slope has the following interpretation, stated correctly:

· \beta > 1: experimental effects are larger than predicted → underestimation.
· \beta < 1: predicted effects are larger than experimental → overestimation.
· \beta = 1: proportional agreement in the through-origin regression.

The intercept model separates constant offset from proportional miscalibration. The slope alone does not classify a dataset as underpredicted. Under y^{\text{exp}}=\alpha+\beta\hat{y}+\epsilon, the prediction error is

e = y^{\text{exp}}-\hat{y} = \alpha+(\beta-1)\hat{y}.

For \beta>1 and \alpha<0, the fitted line crosses the identity line at

\hat{y}^{*} = \frac{-\alpha}{\beta-1}.

The direction of the error reverses at \hat{y}^{*}. Every calibration model therefore reports \hat{y}^{*} with a confidence interval, the fraction of observations on each side, and region-wise underprediction fractions. No dataset is described as "underpredicted" from \beta alone.

Metric separation is mandatory. DA, \rho_s, and magnitude metrics measure different properties. DA does not establish magnitude accuracy. \rho_s does not establish proportional agreement. auROC is reported only where there is a pre-specified binary classification task, never as a proxy for direction accuracy.

3.2 Layer II — Contextual reliability

Layer II evaluates stability across cell-state contexts, modality availability, sampling and subsampling variation, genomic distance, and experimental datasets.

Provenance — operationalized via filter_to_observed.

The v2.0.0 API release notes confirm a filter_to_observed helper on TrackData and JunctionData. This is the operational primitive. It is not a per-prediction metadata field; it is a filtering function that restricts track data to observed (non-imputed) entries. The provenance audit (G1.4, §5 below) uses it as follows:

1. Query with full observed inputs. Apply filter_to_observed. If it returns a strict subset of tracks, the complement is the imputed set — provenance is operationalizable.
2. Query with partially observed inputs. Re-apply filter_to_observed. The difference in surviving track sets relative to the full-observation case is the empirical provenance estimate.
3. Pass criterion: filter_to_observed produces a stable, non-empty observed subset across test loci and cell states.
4. Fail criterion: it returns all tracks (no filtering capability), returns empty, or raises an error. In that case provenance is unverified, and H3 downgrades to observational IRG with user-side tracking only.

Observational provenance gap.

\mathrm{IRG}_{m,c} = \mathbb{E}[S\mid I_{m,c}=1] - \mathbb{E}[S\mid I_{m,c}=0],

with adjustment for dataset, cell-state composition, measurement quality, sample size, and effect-size distribution. This is explicitly observational, not causal.

Controlled Modality Ablation Effect (CMAE). Where the API supports withholding a modality and triggering the intended imputation pathway:

\mathrm{CMAE}_{m,c} = \mathbb{E}_{v\sim\mathcal{V}_c}\!\left[S_{\text{obs}}(v,c,m)-S_{\text{mask}}(v,c,m)\right].

CMAE is interpreted as the causal effect of withholding a model input on the model's output or reliability, under fixed inference conditions. It is not a claim that the biological modality causally improves prediction. Two mandatory validity checks:

· C1 (valid imputation, not out-of-distribution collapse). Verify that masking activates the intended imputation path, and that the masked-output distribution does not collapse or lie outside the observed-input distribution. If C1 fails, CMAE is reported as not interpretable.
· C2 (held-constant conditions). Variant set, cell state, pinned model version (v2.0.0), and scoring function are held fixed. Any deviation is logged as a separate condition.

If C1 fails for a modality, the observational IRG is retained with full covariate adjustment, and CMAE is marked unavailable.

3.3 Layer III — Experimental utility

E(v) = w_1\,\mathrm{Stability}(v) + w_2\,\mathrm{Concordance}(v) + w_3\,\mathrm{Provenance}(v) - w_4\,\mathrm{DistanceUncertainty}(v).

Component definitions, normalization procedures, stochastic-pass seeds, primary-modality assignments, and weights are frozen on development data only before evaluation-set predictions are inspected. Evaluation data are never used to construct or tune the score.

Two utility endpoints, pre-specified.

(i) Validation yield.

\mathrm{HitRate}@k(S) = \frac{1}{k}\sum_{v\in \mathrm{TopK}(S)} \mathbb{1}[Y_v^{\text{exp}}\text{ meets the criterion}].

Where the baseline hit rate is positive:

\mathrm{ER}@k = \frac{\mathrm{HitRate}@k(E)}{\mathrm{HitRate}@k(S_{\text{raw}})},

and the absolute difference is always reported:

\Delta@k = \mathrm{HitRate}@k(E)-\mathrm{HitRate}@k(S_{\text{raw}})

with a bootstrap confidence interval. If the baseline hit rate is zero, only \Delta@k is reported; the ratio is marked undefined, not assigned a convention.

(ii) Biological utility. A pre-specified magnitude or relevance endpoint with fixed direction (e.g., mean |\log\text{FC}| among validated selections). Fixed before the ablation is run.

Diversity constraint. All selection strategies — raw score, evidence score, random — use identical constraints: at most 2 variants per gene, and at most 3 per 100 kb window. Both constrained and unconstrained results are reported.

