4. Two independent tracks

4.1 Track A — Regulatory prediction validation

Track A evaluates model outputs against experimental variant-effect estimands: eQTL, caQTL, sQTL, MPRA. The central requirement is estimand compatibility between the model output and the experimental measurement. Track A is the direct setting for direction, ranking, magnitude and calibration.

Dataset eligibility (G1.2). GTEx and TenK10K are classified C₃ (exposure during model development or benchmark evaluation) and excluded from confirmatory H1. AlphaGenome 2 is trained on bulk and single-cell data including GTEx and related resources, and TenK10K is the primary developer-reported benchmark. Using either for confirmatory out-of-distribution distance-calibration claims would violate independence. Both remain eligible for in-distribution performance checks only.

4.2 Track B — Perturbation-response validation

Track B investigates whether model-derived regulatory predictions anticipate experimental perturbation responses: CRISPRi enhancer ablation, CRISPRa, TAP-seq, and Perturb-seq where biological conditions are compatible.

The non-equivalence is explicit. A variant-effect prediction and an enhancer-ablation response are not equivalent quantities. Track B uses a finite, pre-specified set of candidate transformations, selected on development data only:

1. identity (naive baseline, expected to fail but necessary as a reference);
2. monotonic rank transformation;
3. linear transformation fitted on development data;
4. biologically motivated sigmoid saturation;
5. no transformation — prioritization only, with quantitative calibration explicitly disclaimed.

If none of transformations 1–4 achieves defensible out-of-sample performance (pre-specified: out-of-sample R^2 < 0.1 or calibration slope outside [0.5, 2]), Track B is restricted to prioritization. The fallback is pre-specified to prevent post-hoc transformation shopping.

