12. Falsification principle

AG2-PACT is not designed to demonstrate that its evidence layer must improve AlphaGenome 2. It is designed to test whether it does.

Three outcomes are possible:

· Positive: reproducible improvement beyond raw model scores, with identifiable component contributions.
· Negative or equivalent: no improvement at scientifically meaningful effect sizes, or equivalence to baseline within the pre-specified margin.
· Inconclusive: dataset, power, API or independence limitations prevent a defensible conclusion.

All three are scientifically reportable. A framework that cannot demonstrate that its own contribution is unnecessary is not an adequate validation framework. The preregistration (G1.3b) and the component ablation (H4) are the concrete mechanisms by which the project binds itself to the possibility of a null result.

---

13. Conclusion

AG2-PACT proposes an independent framework for evaluating the reliability and experimental utility of conditional genomic foundation models. Its premise is that predictive accuracy, biological context, modality provenance, genomic distance, and experimental prioritization are related but distinct dimensions, and that a conditional model's aggregate benchmark performance does not establish reliability under perturbation or in unseen cellular contexts.

The research separates regulatory variant-effect validation (Track A) from perturbation-response evaluation (Track B), operationalizes provenance through the filter_to_observed API primitive, tests distance-dependent calibration with equivalence-based inference and small-cluster-robust uncertainty, and evaluates whether structured evidence improves experimental selection through a pre-specified component ablation that can refuse to attribute utility to provenance.

The immediate priority is not implementation. It is establishing whether a sufficiently independent, biologically compatible, statistically adequate, and legally usable evidence base exists — and G1.3b preregistration is the gateway.

Measure the evidence base first; build the instrument second.

