Appendix H — Epistemic Principles Distilled

These are the principles that emerged from the session and now govern the programme. They are not decoration; each was earned by a specific correction (cross-referenced to Appendix C).

H.1 On measurement

P-1. A metric measures what it measures, not what we want it to measure. ρ_s measures rank, not magnitude (F-02). DA measures sign, not discrimination (F-03). β measures proportional calibration, not absolute error (M-03). Conflating them is not a simplification; it is a category error.

P-2. A slope alone does not classify a dataset. Under y^{\text{exp}} = \alpha + \beta\hat{y}, the sign of the error depends on \hat{y}. Report the crossing point, the fraction on each side, and the region-wise error (M-03).

P-3. No non-significant result is proof of no effect. Equivalence must be tested, not inferred from absence of significance (M-04, M-05).

H.2 On experimental design

P-4. Perturbation ≠ variation. A CRISPRi ablation and a DNA variant are different biological quantities. Treating the difference as calibration error is a category error dressed as a metric (M-01).

P-5. A transformation must be pre-specified, finite, and falsifiable. Otherwise the “transformation” is a post-hoc fit to the evaluation set (P-03).

P-6. Dataset selection is a scientific eligibility decision, not a convenience. A public dataset is not automatically licensed, independent, or out-of-distribution (F-04, S-02).

H.3 On statistics

P-7. Small-cluster inference requires small-cluster methods. Asymptotic cluster-robust SEs are invalid for G < 40; the “rule of 42” is unreliable for unbalanced clusters (M-06).

P-8. A difference in significance is not a significant difference. Comparing AG1 and AG2 requires Δγ₃ with clustered bootstrap CI, not two separate p-values (M-05).

P-9. Leakage is silent. If the stochastic seed, primary modality, k, or normalization constants are chosen after seeing evaluation data, the metric is partially in-sample and no correction can recover it (M-07).

H.4 On scientific honesty

P-10. The framework must be able to demonstrate its own irrelevance. A framework that can only confirm its utility is a persuasion device, not a scientific instrument (R-06, FR-17).

P-11. Negative, equivalent, and inconclusive results are all reportable. The three-outcome structure of H1 and H4 exists precisely so that no result is forced into a false binary (M-04, M-05).

P-12. Pre-registration is the cheapest defense against post-hoc drift. It converts “a framework we built” into “a hypothesis we tested” (S-07, FR-10).

P-13. Causal language must be scoped to the intervention actually performed. CMAE is a claim about model input, not about biology (M-02).

H.5 On research programme discipline

P-14. Measure the evidence base first; build the instrument second. No code until G1.1–G1.5 are frozen (R-04).

P-15. Verification against primary sources is not optional. Six factual errors (F-01 to F-06) were caught by source verification. Without it, the programme would have been built on a reversed slope, a mislabeled dataset, and a non-existent API field.

P-16. Every correction must be logged, not overwritten. The intellectual trajectory is part of the scientific record (this appendix).

