Appendix K — Summary of the Programme’s Central Commitment

Across all corrections, one principle remained constant and was sharpened rather than revised:

AG2-PACT is not designed to demonstrate that its evidence layer must improve AlphaGenome 2. It is designed to test whether it does — and to report, without distortion, the outcome either way.

Every structural decision in v0.3 is downstream of this commitment:

· the three-part H1 decision (support / equivalence / inconclusive) exists so that no result is forced into a false binary;
· the H4 equivalence clause exists so that a small-but-real effect is not misreported as null;
· the 10-row component ablation exists so that provenance can be denied credit if it does not earn it;
· the pre-registration gate exists so that the evidence score cannot be tuned to the evaluation set;
· the filter_to_observed fallback exists so that a missing API primitive cannot be replaced by a plausible-sounding proxy;
· the Track A / Track B separation exists so that a category error cannot be dressed as a calibration metric;
· the contamination audit exists so that a zero-shot claim is not contaminated by training data.

The programme’s central falsifiable question, restated once more for the record:

Can cell-state-aware evidence provenance and distance-dependent reliability assessment improve the experimental validation efficiency of conditional genomic foundation-model predictions beyond raw sequence-to-function scores?

If the answer is yes, the contribution is the evidence layer. If the answer is no, the contribution is the demonstration that the layer is unnecessary — which is equally scientific, and which the framework is explicitly designed to be able to report.

